p 1.5-1 Base rates** (external literature; [Estimate]):\n- 5-year survival of mega-cap ERP incumbent: >95%\n- Mega-cap software firm sustaining >10% revenue CAGR for 5 years: ~25\u201335%\n- On-prem\u2192cloud platform transition success: ~20\u201330% for the average firm \u2014 **but SAP is already 57% cloud (FY2025) and guided to 63\u201364% cloud (FY2026), with cloud gross margin 74.6% non-IFRS** [Actual]. The transition is substantially completed, not hoped-for.\n- Enterprise software suite switching rate: ~5%/yr; ERP replacement is a multi-year, high-risk program.\n\n**Step 1.5-2 Deviation from base rate:** My base+bull scenario probability is ~70% vs the ~30% base rate for mega-cap double-digit growth. Justification (3+ company-specific):\n1. Current Cloud Backlog (CCB) \u20ac22.93B at Q2 2026, +27% actual and +26% cc [Actual] \u2014 contracted, short-dated (next-12-month) revenue visibility of unique quality.\n2. Total cloud backlog \u20ac77B, +30% cc at Q4 2025 [Actual] \u2014 multi-year contracted revenue, already signed.\n3. ECC (legacy ERP) end-of-maintenance after 2027 [Estimate \u2014 widely published] \u2192 forced multi-year migration wave to S/4HANA cloud; SAP is the default incumbent for ~2/3 of large-enterprise ERP [Estimate].\n4. Q4 2025: Business AI attached to 2/3 of cloud order entry [Actual \u2014 management statement on Q4 call].\n\n**Step 1.5-3 Base-rate shock check:** Success probability ~70% vs base rate ~30% \u2192 ratio >2.0\u00d7 \u2192 mandatory re-examination. Re-examination result: the 70% is justified by contracted backlog (Tier-1 accounting evidence), not by TAM narrative. However, I am applying a Bayesian haircut (Section 4.3) reducing posterior to ~60%. This passes the red-flag test.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n**Step 2-1 Business understanding (3 sentences):** SAP sells mission-critical enterprise resource planning (S/4HANA), financials, supply-chain, HR and spend-management software to the world\u2019s largest companies, delivered predominantly as cloud subscriptions (RISE with SAP / GROW with SAP). It monetizes a half-century installed base by migrating customers from perpetual licenses into the cloud, attaching Business AI (Joule, Business AI platform, Autonomous Enterprise suite) to raise ARPU. Its business model is highly recurring (86% \u201cmore predictable revenue\u201d FY2025 [Actual]) with pricing power from 50-year lock-in and regulated-industry specialization.\n**Understandability:** High.\n\n**Step 2-2 Moat assessment (0\u20133 each):**\n| Moat type | Score | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets | 3 | Stable |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Stable |\n| Network effects | 2 | Widening (partner ecosystem) |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Stable |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Stable |\n| Data moat | 2 | Widening (AI training on process data) |\n| **Total** | **15/21** | **Wide, Stable-to-Widening** |\n\nWidening signals: CCB +26% cc; AI attached to \u2154 of Q4 cloud orders; defense segment fastest-growing [Estimate \u2014 CEO, Mar 2026]. Narrowing risks: Oracle in HCM/Financials, AI-native workflow tools, mid-market cloud-native ERPs.\n\n**Step 2-3 Lynch classification:** Fast Grower at scale (cloud growing 26% cc) with Stalwart characteristics (recurring revenue, 50-yr franchise). Checkpoints: institutional ownership \u2014 high; balance sheet \u2014 net cash ~\u20ac1B; PEG \u2014 1.8 [Aggregator] \u2192 PEG \u2248 1.8 acceptable for quality.\n\n**Step 2-4 Financial strength scorecard (FY2025 [Actual unless tagged]):**\n- Revenue: \u20ac36.80B, +8% actual, +11% cc (FY2024: \u20ac34.18B)\n- Cloud revenue: \u20ac21.02B, +26% cc; Cloud ERP Suite +32% cc\n- Operating margin: 28.3% non-IFRS (26.7% IFRS); +260bp y/y\n- EPS: non-IFRS \u20ac6.15, +36%; IFRS \u20ac6.28 (2024 distorted by impairments)\n- ROE: ~18\u201320% [Estimate \u2014 vendor 18.32% plausible at equity \u2248 \u20ac40B]\n- FCF margin: 22.4% (\u20ac8.24B FCF / \u20ac36.80B); FCF/OCF = 90%\n- Net debt/EBITDA \u2248 0.1\u00d7; net cash \u2248 +\u20ac0.9B (vendor cash \u20ac9.66B \u2212 vendor debt \u20ac8.78B) [Aggregator]\n- Interest coverage: >15\u00d7 [Estimate]\n- **Judgment: Excellent.**\n\n**Step 2-5 Capital allocation scorecard (15 pts):**\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 \u20ac10B 2-yr program announced Jan 2026 [Actual]; executed at P/E ~25\u201330\u00d7, not trough valuations.\n2. M&A: 2/3 \u2014 Dremio, Prior Labs (AI/Data), WalkMe; bolt-on, strategically coherent; integration unproven.\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 asset-light; capex \u2248 \u20ac1.1B on \u20ac37B revenue [Estimate].\n4. Mix rationality: 3/3 \u2014 reinvest in cloud/AI + buyback + dividend.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 FY2025: met revenue, beat non-IFRS op profit and FCF outlook [Actual].\nInsider bonus: 0 (insider 0.0% per pack; founder stake not reliably disclosed in pack).\n**Total: 13/15 \u2014 Strong.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n- **R&D capitalization [Estimate]:** R&D \u2248 \u20ac7.5B (\u224820% of revenue). Capitalize 50%, amortize over 4 years \u2192 Adjusted op profit \u2248 IFRS op profit \u20ac9.83B + \u20ac3.75B \u2212 \u20ac2.5B \u2248 **\u20ac11.1B**.\n- **Adjusted invested capital [Estimate]:** Equity \u2248 \u20ac40B + debt \u20ac9B \u2212 cash \u20ac10B + unamortized R&D asset \u2248 \u20ac12B \u2192 **\u20ac51B**.\n- **Adjusted NOPAT [Estimate]:** \u20ac11.1B \u00d7 (1\u221229% tax) \u2248 \u20ac7.9B \u2192 **Adjusted ROIC \u2248 15\u201316%**. GAAP ROIC \u2248 18% (IC \u20ac39B; NOPAT \u20ac7.0B). Gap <5pp \u2192 no material distortion.\n- **SBC handling:** Vendor SBC $1.916B \u2248 \u20ac1.75B [Estimate; period unreliable]. SAP\u2019s reported FCF already nets cash SBC settlements within OCF (management noted \u201clower payouts for share-based compensation\u201d driving FCF [Actual]). I therefore do NOT double-deduct SBC from company-reported FCF, but I disclose it: conservative owner earnings below.\n- **Owner Earnings (Buffett-style) [Estimate]:** Net income \u20ac7.49B + D&A \u20ac2.5B \u2212 maintenance capex \u20ac1.2B \u2212 \u0394WC \u20ac0.3B \u2212 full SBC \u20ac1.75B \u2248 **\u20ac6.7B**. This is the conservative floor.\n- Note: Reported FCF \u20ac8.24B > owner earnings because D&A exceeds capex and deferred revenue (from cloud growth) inflates OCF.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (Engine 1: Traditional DCF + Multiples)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n**Step 0-1/0-2/0-4 Engine selection:** Archetype B \u2014 Quality Compounder. SAP is not cyclical (subscription model); not option-led (AI upside is embedded in signed backlog, not priced as separate optionality); not binary. No archetype transition: the on-prem\u2192cloud shift is a *business-model* transition, but the financial profile is already that of a SaaS compounder (cloud 57% of revenue, recurring 86%, FCF record). Engine 1 correct; Errors if using Engine 4: would double-count AI optionality already contracted in backlog.\n\n**Discount rate derivation [Assumption/Estimate]:**\n- Risk-free: 3.0% (blend of Bund 2.2% and USD global pricing)\n- ERP: 4.5%\n- Levered beta: 0.90 (pack beta 0.757 deemed unreliable \u2014 window/benchmark unknown; historical 5-yr beta ~0.9 [Estimate])\n- Cost of equity: 3.0 + (0.90 \u00d7 4.5) = 7.05%\n- Pre-tax debt: 3.4% (SAP\u2019s May 2026 \u20ac3.5B Eurobond [Actual]); after-tax at 29%: 2.4%\n- Weights: ~99% equity / ~1% debt (net debt \u2248 \u20ac1B)\n- **WACC \u2248 7.0%.** Valuation conservatively uses **7.5%**.\n\n**Explicit forecast (base case, EUR billions)** \u2014 2026 uses guided figures; 2027+ [Assumption] with sources stated:\n| FY | 