uts USD-reported growth and ADR returns; FY2026 guidance embeds ~8\u201310pp FX headwind.\n4. **Margin-peak risk:** 36.5% terminal margin assumption requires AI-driven efficiency to outrun AI investment (Dremio/Prior Labs dilution \u2248 \u20ac0.1B already visible [Actual]).\n5. **Regulatory/legal:** EU licensing/aftermarket-services commitments; US federal price-fixing probe; prior $222M bribery settlement (2026) [Estimate \u2014 research brief].\n6. **Competition:** Oracle in HCM/Financials; Microsoft Dynamics in mid-market.\n\n**Catalysts:**\n- Q3/Q4 2026 CCB prints (expect +20\u201328% cc)\n- FY2026 results (Feb 2027): FCF \u2265 \u20ac10B, margin ~30%\n- AI monetization disclosures (Business AI attach rate, Autonomous Suite pipeline)\n- Defense-sector expansion (fastest-growing business [Estimate \u2014 CEO] \u2014 European re-armament)\n- \u20ac10B buyback execution through Dec 2027 [Actual]\n- ECC end-of-maintenance 2027 migration wave\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n- SAP\u2019s tail correlation with Nasdaq-100 \u2248 0.75 [Assumption]; with EU equities \u2248 0.8. In a 2022-style regime (rates up, equities down), SAP likely \u221235\u201345% [Estimate].\n- Portfolio impact at 7% weight: a \u221240% SAP drawdown costs \u22122.8% portfolio.\n- CVaR (5%, 1-year): \u2248 \u221245% for SAP [Assumption] \u2192 CVaR contribution \u2248 \u22123.2% portfolio at 7% weight.\n- No shared bear trigger with a typical US tech portfolio except rates; SAP\u2019s EUR exposure is a partial diversifier if USD weakens (but then SAP\u2019s EUR revenue translation helps the ADR only partially).\n- Verdict: acceptable at 7%; avoid adding at >8% in a rate-sensitive portfolio.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 8 \u2014 MONITORING RULES & THESIS INTEGRITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n**Thesis integrity KPIs (quarterly):**\n| KPI | Confirm | Alert | Kill/Re-evaluate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CCB growth (cc) | >20% | 12\u201315% | <10% for 2 quarters |\n| Cloud revenue growth (cc) | >18% | 12\u201315% | <10% |\n| non-IFRS operating margin | >29% | 26\u201328% | <25% |\n| FCF (FY2026) | \u2265\u20ac10.0B | \u20ac8.5\u20139.0B | <\u20ac8.0B |\n| Total cloud backlog (Q4\u201926) | >\u20ac85B | \u20ac75\u201380B | <\u20ac70B |\n| EUR/USD | <1.15 | 1.15\u20131.25 | >1.30 (ADR return hit) |\n\n**Re-evaluation triggers:** Deteriorating CCB conversion (revenue growth < CCB growth by >5pp); loss of a Fortune-100 core migration to Oracle; EU/US regulatory fine >\u20ac1B; management guide-down on FY2027.\n\n**PRE-MORTEM (Step 7-4) \u2014 \u201cIf this fails by 2030, why?\u201d**\n1. **AI unbundling:** Large customers run ERP data through external AI orchestration layers; SAP\u2019s suite premium collapses. Early warning: CCB growth <15% while competitor AI adoption surges.\n2. **Migration completion:** By 2029 the ECC wave is done; cloud growth falls to ~5\u20136% without AI ARPU offset. Early warning: Cloud ERP Suite growth decelerating toward 15% while Business AI attach stays <50%.\n3. **Regulatory open-interfaces mandate** (EU): forces API-level competition, erodes suite lock-in. Early warning: EC commitments expanded into structural remedies.\n4. **FX/WACC shock:** EUR/USD \u2192 1.30 and 10Y \u2192 5%: WACC 9%, IV drops toward \u20ac185\u2013200.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 9 \u2014 RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n**Step 9-1 Short thesis (5\u20137 bullets, using same data):**\n1. SAP\u2019s \u201crecord backlog\u201d is not all contracted revenue: termination-for-convenience clauses (disclosed in Q4 2025 [Actual]) and long-dated ramps mean \u20ac77B overstates near-term convertibility.\n2. The cloud migration is a pull-forward, not a perpetuum mobile: when the ECC installed base (~2/3 of large-enterprise ERP) finishes migrating by 2028\u20132029, cloud growth falls to the mid-single digits, leaving a 27\u00d7 multiple stranded.\n3. AI agents on top of your ERP data (OpenAI/Anthropic + Palantir-style integration) can replace the \u201cintelligent suite\u201d narrative; SAP\u2019s AI attach (\u2154 of Q4 orders) is a promotional metric, not proven ARPU.\n4. FY2026 guidance embeds an ~8\u201310pp FX headwind \u2014 the EUR strength that crushes reported USD growth is just beginning.\n5. Regulatory stack is thickening: EU aftermarket-services commitments + US price-fixing probe + the 2026 $222M bribery settlement [Estimate] = rising compliance drag.\n6. Margin expansion to 36%+ assumes AI-driven hiring/need discipline holds for a decade \u2014 historically SAP margins were 20\u201328% and restructuring was recurring.\n7. Inside the pack itself, the vendor balance sheet cross-checks fail (P/B 65\u00d7 vs ROE 18%) \u2014 a reminder that the data layer on this name is thinner than a software mega-cap should have.\n\n**Step 9-2 Logic combat:** Long logic 4/5 (contracted backlog, duopoly, 86% recurring) vs Short logic 3/5 (AI disruption unproven; FX is translation, not destruction). Gap = 1 \u2192 no conviction downgrade.\n\n**Step 9-3 Adversarial data:** No current Hindenburg-style report found [Unconfirmed]. The bear case rests on structural tech transition and multi-year deceleration, not on fraud/misrepresentation.\n\n**Step 9-4 Killed arguments:**\n- *\u201cOracle will displace SAP in core ERP\u201d* \u2014 killed: replacement risk is existential for customers; duopoly reshuffles only at the margin. Revives if SAP begins losing Fortune-100 core deals.\n- *\u201cAI makes ERP obsolete\u201d* \u2014 killed (partially): process data inside a regulated, audited system remains the moat; LLMs create the UI, not the system of record. Revives if agents can operate across a fragmented stack without the ERP as backbone.\n- *\u201cDefense segment w